0b7dab125" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048964}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522708}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Natural Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "d60c198f-7395-53e7-9b80-37df76e919d2" :step/math-academy-id 14409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088512}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087692 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087497 :question/id #uuid "37ca3b87-9f5f-583c-95ff-83ca35608559" :question/math-academy-id "e-4155"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "169ca81a-c698-553c-9f47-d6c4303b82a9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522777}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Natural Logarithms of Fractions and Roots"} :step/id #uuid "2d52a820-958f-501f-b903-bbb1725d6a6c" :step/math-academy-id 15037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088888 :tutorial/content "**Euler’s number**, denoted $e,$ is one of the most important constants in mathematics. It's an irrational number, and its numerical value is approximately\n\n$$\ne\\approx2.718\\,281\\,828.\n$$\n\nThe **natural logarithm**, denoted $\\ln,$ is a special logarithm whose base is equal to Euler's number:\n\n$$\n\\ln a = \\log_e a\n$$\n\nIn other words, the natural logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\ln{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = e^x$\n\nEverything we've done with logarithms can be applied to the natural logarithm.\n\nFor instance, we can express $e^3 \\approx 20.086$ in logarithmic form. If we compare $e^{3} \\approx 20.086$ with $a = e^x,$ we get $a = 20.086$ and $x = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\ne^3\\approx 20.086\\quad  \\textrm{is equivalent to}\\quad \\ln(20.086) \\approx 3.\n$$\n\nNote:\n\n- Scientific calculators usually have a special key that allows $\\ln a$ to be computed for specific values of $a.$\n\n- Euler's number shows up in many different areas, including compound interest, population growth, and calculus. For now, it’s enough to know that $e$ is a natural base for logarithms and exponentials. We’ll meet this number often in future lessons, where its deeper significance will become clear." :tutorial/id #uuid "b07e75a3-c350-5268-ad01-a8b5fb789147" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2868 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "89fbb86b-6ce2-55ba-a735-e867abf24645" :step/math-academy-id 15308 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088502}}] :activity/title "The Natural Logarithm" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087902}]}
